{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "ice_age_3d",
    "description": "PISM ice-age model (AgeModel) 3-D block component (stage 2 of the eqice project): the age PDE d(tau)/dt + u*d(tau)/dx + v*d(tau)/dy + w*d(tau)/dz = 1 solved on a 7x7-column block of the 31x31 test-F grid (stage1/dumps/age), on the 62-level fine vertical grid of columnSystemCtx (dz = Lz/61; level k of the fine grid sits between storage levels k-1 and k of the 61-level storage grid, the fixed linear coarse_to_fine map). The full 31x31 field cannot be integrated by the ODE harness: its dense Jacobian over ~119k unknowns needs ~113 GB (measured and killed in an early probe), so this component follows the documented sub-domain pattern of the energy-3D sibling (stage2/ice_energy_3d.esm): a 7x7-column block (~9k unknowns, ~0.7 GB Jacobian) evolved as a coupled PDE with LIVE horizontal exchange between its columns and block-edge ghosts frozen at the full-field initial age -- exactly zero in this run (age.initial_value = 0), which is numerically identical to the C++'s horizontal term over this run because the C++ evaluates the horizontal stencil at the OLD time from the step-start neighbor ages, all zero (the difference is the integrated horizontal contribution of the developing age field, measured and pinned in the tests). The block forcing is the full-field test-F velocity and surface fields sliced to the block: the block-local parameters u3_b, v3_b, w3_b (63-entry padded storage columns per block cell; entry k = storage level max(k-1, 1) for k = 1..62, entry 63 = the top storage level, so the fine-grid bracket reads of age_coarse_to_fine are plain in-bounds gathers at every fine level) and ks_b (the fine-grid surface level index per block cell, 1-based: the C++ ColumnSystemCtx::init_column m_ks + 1 = floor(H/dz) + 1, capped at 62; 1 for ice-free cells). The defaults are the dome block (C++ cells 13..19 x 13..19) and each test overrides the four block slices through parameter_overrides (the same per-test override pattern as the single-column AgeColumn model, stage2/ice_age.esm) -- the energy-3D sibling's i0/j0 full-field gather form (read the full field at the absolute esm indices (i0+i, j0+j)) is documented as the stage-3 embedding, but the native compiler refuses parameter-indexed gathers in any rule (its error: index: axis 0 is neither an affine/wrap map of an unclaimed output symbol nor a constant select; probed exhaustively: parameter gathers must carry literal offsets), and the interpreter fallback measures >40 min per one-step test, so this component ships the native block-slice form, which is physically identical. This component closes the deferred 3-D piece of the single-column AgeColumn model (stage2/ice_age.esm). The vertical machinery is the AgeColumn model's, imported by reference through ./age_templates.esm (age_vertical_advection, age_horizontal_advection, age_storage_interpolate) and ./age3d_templates.esm (age_coarse_to_fine, age_horizontal_advection_field): first-order upwind, implicit in the vertical (an ODE term here, integrated by the harness's stiff solver), explicit in the horizontal; the surface Dirichlet tau = 0 at the fine-grid surface level ks (the C++ row m_ks), the basal accumulation tau = 0 where the bed velocity is strictly upward (the C++ AA > 0 row 0), and tau = 0 at every level above the surface (the C++ x[k] = 0 for k > m_ks). The state tau is the fine-grid age (seconds; the C++ x[k] of the tridiagonal solve), hadv the horizontal advection term u*d(tau)/dx + v*d(tau)/dy at each fine level (first-order upwind, current-time, coupled to the live neighbor columns of the block; the new 3-D coupling of this component), and age_storage the solution interpolated back to the 61-level storage (dump) grid (AgeModel::fine_to_coarse, the fixed linear-in-level weights (k-1)/60). Tests integrate ONE dt = 315569259.747 s step from age = 0 in six blocks placed to cover the dome center, the mid-radius columns at several azimuths, the near-margin columns, and ice-free cells, and assert age_storage against the dumped age_out (stage1/dumps/age/columns.csv) at block-local coordinates, plus pins of the model's own measured hadv (the horizontal contribution the C++'s old-time treatment omits over this step). The C++ takes one backward-Euler step with the horizontal term frozen at the old time (zero here, since the whole domain starts at age 0); the harness integrates the ODE continuously, so the asserted agreement carries the exact-ODE-vs-BE vertical scheme difference of the single-column component (<= 9e-5 relative deep and mid, up to ~6e-3 at the two storage levels just below the dome surface) PLUS the integrated horizontal contribution of the developing age field (small for one step because the deep field is horizontally uniform at dt while the near-surface gradients advect with |u| ~ 1e-9..1e-8 m/s over dx = 58 km). Asserted at rel 1e-3 (deep and mid) and rel 1e-2 (the storage levels adjacent to each column's surface). Deferred to stage 3: computing ks from the ice thickness in-model (here a parameter, as in the single-column component), the coarse_to_fine of the age field itself (the C++ interpolates the old-time age before the horizontal read; here the fine-grid state is evolved directly), coupling the velocities from the SIA/SSA stress balance instead of the analytic test-F forcing, the full 31x31 field (infeasible for the harness's dense Jacobian; the block model is the documented sub-domain pattern), and the energy-3D sibling's i0/j0 full-field gather form (refused by the native compiler; the block-slice form is physically identical and native).",
    "license": "GPL-3.0-or-later",
    "tags": [
      "eqice:stage2",
      "pism:age",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_2d"
    ]
  },
  "solver": {
    "stiffness": "high",
    "reltol": 1e-08,
    "abstol": 1e-08
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "i": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 7
    },
    "j": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 7
    },
